  [PASS] 83d (FORECAST) the aligned quadrupole is within reach of data that already exist, but only just, and only if the stack is rotated to each lens's own external-field direction.  On the KiDS-1000 isolated lens errors that are on disk it is a 1-3 sigma effect per mass bin and a few sigma combined -- a go-look, not a discovery in waiting   (combined S/N = 3.8 over the four KiDS mass bins at e_N = 0.01 with the <sin^2 Theta> = 2/3 stacking factor included; the single best bin gives 3.06.  The monopole in the same data is detected at S/N = 29, so this is a 13 per cent measurement of a quadrupole on a signal already in hand)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
mutation controls, and the LambdaCDM alternative
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  MUTATION 1 (external field switched off): quadrupole/monopole at 0.5 Mpc falls from +0.2124 to +0.000000 -- an isolated MOND galaxy's phantom is exactly spherical, so the whole signal is the external field's
  the LambdaCDM alternative, computed with the same code: a SPHERICAL NFW gives quadrupole/monopole = -0.00000 (zero, as it must) and an NFW flattened to q = 0.8 -- about what dark-matter simulations give for the projected shape -- returns +0.0475, roughly 4 times SMALLER than the framework's +0.2124 at e_N = 0.01.  So the amplitude is a discriminant after all -- but the SIGN-DEFINITE one is still the axis, because a halo's ellipticity axis is the light's and the phantom's is the external field's
  [PASS] 83e MUTATION CONTROLS behave, and the discriminant is named: with no external field the phantom is exactly round; a spherical NFW returns exactly zero through the same pipeline; and a realistically flattened NFW returns a quadrupole several times SMALLER than the framework's.  The measurement that matters is still whether the quadrupole's axis follows ghat_ext or the light, because those two axes are only weakly correlated and only one of them is a dark-matter prediction   (no-field quadrupole +0.00000; spherical NFW -0.00000; q = 0.8 NFW +0.0475 against the framework's +0.2124 at e_N = 0.01)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERDICT.  Item 83 is a REAL and REACHABLE test and this run makes it quantitative for the first time.
The phantom's quadrupole is not a small correction: the total potential's l = 2 term is 19 per cent of its
monopole, matching the analytic linearised QUMOND value to a few per cent, and after
projection the tangential DeltaSigma carries a cos 2phi modulation of 0.21 of the monopole at 0.5 Mpc for a
typical field e_N = 0.01, diluted only by <sin^2 Theta> = 2/3 in a stack rotated to each lens's own
ghat_ext.  On the KiDS-1000 isolated-lens errors already on disk that is S/N ~ 3.8 combined.  It is NOT a
measurement here -- the shear catalogue is not on disk -- so this is recorded as NOT RUNNABLE / forecast.
The one thing that must not be lost: an NFW halo flattened to q = 0.8 gives 0.048 through the same
pipeline, 4 times smaller but not zero.  The whole content is in the AXIS.  The framework points
it at the nearest attractor; every dark-matter model points it at the light or at the filament; and the
g_ext vectors needed to rotate the stack are already committed for SPARC and computable for any lens
sample from 2M++.  That is the experiment.
